WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.345
Choosing a model starts with the task.

00:00:02.345 --> 00:00:06.075
A model for casual chat may not be the right choice for coding, summarization,

00:00:06.075 --> 00:00:09.000
structured extraction, document review, or instruction following.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Model size and parameter count give a rough sense of capacity,

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.000
but they do not guarantee quality for every workflow.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.960
A smaller model that fits well and responds quickly may be more useful than a larger model

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:19.000
that barely runs.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.130
Quantization level determines how compact the model file is and how much RAM or VRAM it

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:24.000
may require.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.420
More aggressive quantization can make a model usable on modest hardware,

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:30.000
but it may reduce output quality or change behavior.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.880
The best choice depends on the task, expected quality, latency tolerance, context length,

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:35.230
and available memory.

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:39.255
Testing realistic prompts is better than choosing only by file size.

00:00:39.255 --> 00:00:41.939
Instruction-tuned models and chat templates matter.

00:00:41.939 --> 00:00:45.470
A chat-oriented model is usually trained to respond to role-based messages and instruction

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:45.960
formats.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.760
If the runtime or interface uses the wrong prompt format,

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:51.000
the model may look weaker than it really is.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.830
Context length also matters because long document workflows need more room than short

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:56.000
single-turn prompts.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.370
Model source, provenance,

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:01.000
and licensing should be reviewed before building workflows around a file.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.000
Prefer trusted sources and read model cards when available.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.960
Licensing may affect commercial use, redistribution, modification, or deployment.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:10.000
Privacy needs should also guide model selection.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.000
A local model can keep prompts on your machine,

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.000
but the model file itself is still a dependency that should be tracked and documented.
